// @emit-error an i32-keyed Map/Set cannot be a struct / variant FIELD
// CAPABILITY (B6b): the FIELD position, the LAST one an i32-keyed map cannot occupy.
// Its VALUE type is no longer restricted at all (`i32-keyed-ref-values.vl` runs the
// whole ref/wide set), and the closure-RESULT and MAP-VALUE positions opened with it —
// what is left is the two positions whose ROW erases the key.
//
// A map FIELD records its map's VALUE name in `sFieldElemName` / `uFieldElemName` (and
// its VALUE type in the `sFieldElemTyIx` sidecar), never the `{[K]: V}` spelling: the
// mv slot is keyed on the (KEY, VALUE) PAIR, so a field row that carries only V
// resolves the STRING-keyed twin's map struct — a string-ref keys list read as an i32
// list, which is invalid wasm `vl check` cannot see. Opening it is a KEY COLUMN on both
// field tables written at every `sFieldElemName.push` site (5 in `emit_classify`, 2 in
// `emit_collect`) and read at `sFieldMapValSlot` / `uFieldMapValSlot`; it is filed, not
// guessed, because the parallel-array skew that a half-written column causes is exactly
// the failure class this rep's tables are most fragile to.
//
// The field's own annotation node is a bare `{[i32]: i32}` — a SUPPORTED spelling — so
// `collectA`'s spelling gate cannot see this; only the field ROW knows it sits in a
// struct, which is why the field recorder asks separately and the message names the
// position rather than the key.
type B = { m: {[i32]: i32} }
const b: B = { m: Map() }
b.m[1] = 2
print(b.m.length)
